.TH PNEWS 1 LOCAL
.SH NAME
Pnews - a program for posting news articles
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B Pnews newsgroup title
.br
  or
.br
.B Pnews -h headerfile [oldarticle]
.br
  or
.br
.B Pnews
.SH DESCRIPTION
Pnews is a friendly interface for posting news articles.
It will ask several questions, then allow you to enter your article,
and then post it using the inews(1) program.
If you type h and a carriage return at any point,
.I Pnews
will tell you what it wants to know.
.PP
If you put a
.I .signature
file in your home directory, your inews program will usually append it
to your message after you are done editing it.
If you prefer to see your signature while you are editing, rename your
\&.signature file to be .news_sig and it will be appended before you
begin to edit.
Note that .news_sig obeys the setting of DOTDIR, but .signature may
not, since it is out of Pnews's control.
If both .news_sig and .signature exist you'll get two signatures appended.
.PP
The -h form is used when invoked from
.I trn
or
.IR rn .
If your editor can edit multiple files, and you want the article to which
you are replying to show up as an alternate file, define the environment
variable NEWSPOSTER as \*(L"Pnews -h %h %A\*(R".
You can also modify the the NEWSHEADER environment variable to change the
header file that
.I [t]rn
passes to Pnews.
.PP
Before handing an article to inews(1),
.I Pnews
adds User-Agent, MIME-Version, Content-Type, and Content-Transfer-Encoding
headers.
ASCII-only bodies are declared as 7bit; bodies containing non-ASCII bytes
are declared as 8bit.
.I Pnews
does not otherwise recode the body.
.PP
On Windows, use Pnews.cmd.  Its adjacent Pnews.ps1 runs with Windows
PowerShell 5.1 and incorporates the header-generation workflow.
The installed inews.exe receives the completed article on standard input
with the -h option.  Configure a qualified POSTING_HOSTNAME or the
posting-hostname site file before posting, as required by inews.
Windows does not require a Unix shell or Unix text utilities.
.PP
The Windows composer retains a draft in $DOTDIR/.article.
Abort, end of input, and failed operations also retain it in
$HOME/dead.article.  Child-tool failures return a nonzero status;
a failed submission can be edited and retried before exiting.
The original file supplied with -h is preserved, except for the legacy
trn 4 connection-sharing handoff, which returns status 42.
.PP
Windows mail copies, replies to the special group poster, and unapproved
posts to groups marked moderated require the email backend completed by
the Rnmail port.  Until that backend is available, these requests fail
with a diagnostic and retain the draft before any NNTP submission.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.IP AUTHORCOPY 8
If defined, contains the name of a file to which the finished article will
be appended.
.Sp
Default: article not saved
.IP POSTSAVER 8
Override the author-copy saver command.  The Windows default is the
installed mbox.saver.cmd.  Arguments are the draft path, two dots,
article number zero, offset zero, Pnews, the AUTHORCOPY path, and a
complete From envelope line.  A saver failure after posting is reported
without resubmitting the article.
.IP DOTDIR 8
Where to find your dot files, if they aren't in your home directory.
This is primarily for accounts which are shared by more than one person.
.Sp
Default: $HOME
.IP EDITOR 8
The editor you want to use, if VISUAL is undefined.
.Sp
Default: whatever your news administrator installed, usually vi.
.IP FAST_PNEWS 8
A value beginning with y skips the distribution confirmation and
prepared-file prompt and enters the editor directly.
.IP PAGER 8
The command used by List.  On Windows it must be a native command.
.IP PGPCOMMAND 8
The optional PGP command.  Use Sign, Unsign, or Test from the PGP menu;
the command receives the existing PGP command-line options.
.IP INEWS 8
On Windows, override the submission command.  The installed inews.exe
is the default unless USE_INEWS selected a custom command at configure
time.  Pnews appends -h and streams the message without recoding it.
.IP ARTCHECK 8
On Windows, override the installed trn-artchk.exe validation command.
.IP NNTPLIST 8
On Windows, override the installed nntplist.exe metadata command.
.IP SPELLER 8
On Windows, override the installed Speller.cmd command.
.IP NEWSLIB 8
On Windows, override the configured news library used for distribution
prefixes.  NEWSACTIVE and NEWSDESCRIPTIONS select metadata files;
NETSPEED controls fetching missing files with nntplist.
.PP
Windows tool settings accept a path or a command with whitespace-separated
arguments.  Quote an entire argument with single or double quotes when it
contains spaces.  Shell expressions and redirections are not evaluated.
Tools receiving streamed input or output must be native executables or
PowerShell scripts; console tools can also use batch launchers.
.IP HOME 8
Your home directory.
.Sp
Default: $LOGDIR
.IP LOGDIR 8
Your home directory if HOME is undefined.
.IP LOGNAME 8
Your login name, if USER is undefined.
.Sp
Default: value of \*(L"whoami\*(R".
.IP NAME 8
Your full name.
.Sp
Default: name from /etc/passwd, or ~/.fullname.
.IP NEWSORG 8
Either the name of your organization, or the name of a file containing the
name of your organization. (For use at organizations where the ORGANIZATION
environmental variable is in use for other purposes). If both NEWSORG and
ORGANIZATION are set, NEWSORG will override ORGANIZATION.
.IP ORGANIZATION 8
Either the name of your organization, or the name of a file containing the
name of your organization.
.Sp
Default: whatever your news administrator chose.
.IP REPLYTO 8
The contents of a \*(L"Reply-To:\*(R" header line to insert into your message.
.Sp
Default: header line not inserted.
.IP USER 8
Your login name.
.Sp
Default: $LOGNAME
.IP VISUAL 8
The editor you want to use.
.Sp
Default: $EDITOR
.SH FILES
$DOTDIR/.article
.br
$DOTDIR/.news_sig
.br
~/dead.article
.SH SEE ALSO
trn(1), rn(1), Rnmail(1), inews(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.SH BUGS
Not the speediest program in the world, but maybe that's a blessing to the
net.
